WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.593
Painted panels hang along a wall, and each panel covers a closed span [left, right] of integer positions.

2
00:00:06.593 --> 00:00:13.263
A laser fired straight at integer position x marks every panel whose span contains x, meaning left &lt;=

3
00:00:13.263 --> 00:00:20.506
x &lt;= right. Spans may overlap, touch, be nested or repeat. Return the fewest shots needed so that

4
00:00:20.506 --> 00:00:26.416
every panel is marked at least once. A shot may be fired at any integer position, including one

5
00:00:26.416 --> 00:00:28.025
outside the listed spans.

6
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:36.856
A shot at 2 marks [0, 2] and [-3, 4]; a shot at 10 marks [3, 10] and

7
00:00:36.856 --> 00:00:39.551
[9, 15]; [14, 16]

8
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:49.207
needs its own shot at 16. The spans [0, 2], [3, 10] and [14, 16] are pairwise disjoint,

9
00:00:49.207 --> 00:00:51.207
so three shots are unavoidable.

10
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.494
The spans are pairwise disjoint, so no position lies

11
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.138
in two of them and each needs its own shot.

12
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:13.701
Firing at the smallest right end among unmarked panels marks the most panels any choice for that panel

13
00:01:13.701 --> 00:01:14.085
could.

14
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.958
Copy and sort the panels by right end. Walk them, keeping the last shot position. A panel with

15
00:01:20.958 --> 00:01:25.264
left &gt; lastShot is unmarked: count a shot and fire at its right end.

16
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:30.856
Take the panel with the smallest right end r. Some shot must hit it, and every other panel

17
00:01:30.856 --> 00:01:36.125
ends at or after r, so a panel that contains any shot inside this one also contains r.

18
00:01:36.125 --> 00:01:41.662
Firing at r is therefore never worse, so an exchange argument shows the greedy choice is safe. After

19
00:01:41.662 --> 00:01:48.361
firing, all panels with left &lt;= r are marked; the rest are handled the same way. Sorting by

20
00:01:48.361 --> 00:01:55.176
right end is O(n log n), the scan is O(n), and the sorted copy costs O(n) memory (O(1)

21
00:01:55.176 --> 00:01:57.266
if sorting the input in place is allowed).

22
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:05.312
[0, 2], [-3, 4], [3, 10], [9, 15], [14, 16].

23
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:13.878
left 0 &gt; -infinity so fire at 2; [-3, 4] has left -3 &lt;= 2 and is marked.

24
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:23.400
[3, 10] has left 3 &gt; 2, so fire at 10; [9, 15] is marked. [14, 16] has

25
00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:27.998
left 14 &gt; 10, so fire at 16. Three shots.

26
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:34.782
O(n log n) for the sort, then O(n). O(n) for the sorted copy of the panels
